usage: ../gortt [options] < angles.dat

The first line of the input data reads:
N M W_1 W_2 [...] W_M
where N is the number of view--illumination geometries
M is the number of wavelengths and
W_i (i=1,M) are the wavelengths at which to predict the canopy reflectance
The rest of the input data is four columns of ascii:
view_zenith view_azimuth solar_zenith solar azimuth

The command line options are:

============ Crown geometry options:
-beta arg	force the proportion of mutual shadowing to arg
         	[n.b. if beta is not set gortt uses the model by Li and Strahler (IGARSS'92) to determine mutual shadowing]

------------ EITHER (old style):
-h1 arg    	set the lower boundary of the crown centres (m) to arg
-h2 arg    	set the upper boundary of the crown centres (m) to arg
-b arg     	set the vertical crown radius (m) to arg
-r arg     	set the horizontal crown radius (m) to arg
-lambda arg	set the tree stem density (1/m2) to arg

------------ OR (new style):
-HB  arg	set the ratio of the centroid height range to the vertical crown radius to arg
-BR  arg	set the ratio of the vertical to horizontal crown radius to arg
-PCC arg	set the projected crown cover (at nadir) to arg

The above old style options refer to the original GORT paper and the 
new style options are as they are expressed in the Quaife et al. (2008) 
DALEC paper. As soon as a new style option is specified all old style 
options are ignored. Note that they are same thing - the code simply uses
the new style options to calculate the old ones but the new ones are 
preferred because it reduces equifinality. 

============ Amount of leaf material:
------------ EITHER:
-favd arg	set the foliage area volume density (1/m) within crown to arg

------------ OR:
-LAI  arg	set the leaf area index (m2/m2) for the scene to arg

============ Prospect leaf options:
-N arg  	set the leaf structure variable to arg
-Cab arg	set the leaf chlorophyl content (µg.cm-2) to arg
-Cw arg 	set the equivelant leaf water thickness (cm) to arg
-Car arg 	set the carotenoid content (µg.cm-2) to arg
-Anth arg 	set the anthocyanin content (µg.cm-2) to arg
-Cbrown arg 	set the /brown pigment content (arbitrary units) to arg
-Cm arg 	set the leaf mass per unit area (g.cm-2) to arg

============ Price soil spectra options:
-rsl1 arg 	set the weight of the first soil vector to arg
-rsl2 arg 	set the weight of the second soil vector to arg
-rsl3 arg 	set the weight of the third soil vector to arg
-rsl4 arg 	set the weight of the fourth soil vector to arg

============ User override for spectral properties:
-refl_leaf    arg 	set leaf reflectance to arg (turns prospect off)*
-trans_leaf   arg 	set leaf transmittance to arg (turns prospect off)*
                  	[* n.b. these two must be given together]
-alb_soil     arg 	set soil albedo to arg (turns price off)**
-soil_spectra arg 	read soil spectra from file arg (turns price off)
                  	[** n.b. use only one of the above soil options]

============ Read/write gap probabilities:
-W       	go as far as calculating the gap probabilities and write these to the stdout and exit
-P file  	read gap probabilities from file that have been written using the -W option
         	n.b. the above two options are included to allow fast BRF calculation based on
         	pre-computed gap probabilities. The model must be run using the same crown and canopy
         	geometry options for the read and write. Spectral options (i.e. Prospect and Price options)
         	may be varied whist running using a give probability file.

============ Numerical resolution:
-nlayers arg	set the number of canopy height layers to arg (default 15, minimum 2)
            	this sets the vertical resolution of the whole model, not just the lidar
            	it is the reporting grid only: it does not affect the accuracy of the model
-nquad arg  	multiply the internal quadrature orders by arg (default 1, minimum 1)
            	the default is converged; raise it to check that for a given canopy

============ Lidar options:
-lidar           	predict the lidar waveform instead of the BRF, and exit
                 	n.b. lidar mode does not read stdin at all: the device is assumed to be
                 	at nadir, so no view/illumination geometry is required. Normal output is
                 	suppressed. Cannot be combined with -W, -P or -q08_pn_kopen, all of which
                 	only deal with the gap probabilities at the ground.
-lidar_wl arg    	set the lidar wavelength (nm) to arg (default 1064, must be 400-2500)
-lidar_J0 arg    	set the emitted pulse energy to arg (default 1, a pure scale factor)
-lidar_waveform  	emit the binned waveform rather than the per-layer profile
-lidar_pulse arg 	set the Gaussian pulse width (sigma, m) to arg (default 0, no convolution)
-lidar_binsize arg	set the range bin size (m) to arg (default: the canopy layer thickness)
                 	n.b. the last two apply to -lidar_waveform only
                 	n.b. all of the -lidar_* options require -lidar, and are refused without it

============ Input/output options:
-prnspec	print the scene component spectra for each wavelength inside {}
-prnprop	print the viewed proportions of scene components inside []
-energy 	print the spectral albedo, absoption by veg and absorption by soil for each wavelength after other outputs.
-u      	print this message and exit

